        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it normal for a ceiling fan to wobble a little?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, a small amount is normal. Manufacturers generally treat motor housing movement of around 1/8 inch on high speed as acceptable, and fans on long downrods sway more than flush-mount huggers. Wobble you can see as a clear circle, wobble at low speed, or wobble with clicking or a shifting canopy is beyond normal and worth diagnosing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a wobbling ceiling fan fall?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Wobble from blade imbalance alone rarely brings a fan down, but it does loosen screws over time. The real risk is wobble caused by a loose bracket or an electrical box that is not rated for fan support, because those can pull free. If the box or bracket moves when you push the stationary fan, stop using it until it is fixed."
          }
        },
