    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A ceiling fan that wobbles is almost always fixable with a screwdriver, a yardstick and a two-dollar balancing kit. But check the mount and the electrical box before you touch the blades, because those are the two causes that can bring a 20-pound spinning fan down, and balancing a fan on a failing mount just hides the warning sign.
        </p>

        <div id="diagnostic-order" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Diagnostic Order, and How Much Wobble Is Normal
          </h2>

          <p>
            A ceiling fan wobbles because its spinning mass is not centred under its pivot point, or because the thing holding it up is moving. That gives you eight realistic causes: a loose mounting bracket or canopy, an electrical box that was never rated to carry a fan, loose blade screws, a bent blade iron, blades sitting at different heights, a weight imbalance between blades, a downrod or hanger ball that is not seated properly, and, rarely, a warped blade or a defective motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The order you check them in matters more than people expect. The instinct is to reach for the balancing kit that came in the box, because blade imbalance is the cause everyone has heard of. But a balancing kit can quiet the symptom of a loose bracket or an overloaded box well enough that you stop noticing it, and that is precisely the failure you most need to notice. So the rule is simple: mount first, box second, blade hardware third, blade geometry fourth, blade weight last.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before you start, decide whether you actually have a problem. Fan makers treat a small amount of movement as normal. GE's fan manuals, for example, ask for blade-to-ceiling measurements within 1/8 inch (about 3 mm) of each other, and a housing that moves around 1/8 inch at high speed is generally considered acceptable. A fan on a long downrod will always sway a little more than a hugger, because the same imbalance has a longer lever to swing on. If you can see the motor housing describing a visible circle, hear clicking, or see the canopy shifting against the ceiling, that is beyond normal.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also helps to notice when the wobble appears. Wobble that shows up only on high and is barely visible on low is classically a blade weight or tracking problem, because imbalance forces rise sharply with speed. Wobble that is there at every speed, or that you can produce by gently pushing the motor housing with the fan off, points to the mount, the box or the downrod. A fan that was fine for years and started wobbling recently usually has something that worked loose, often after a vigorous cleaning or a seasonal direction change, which is why our guide to <a href="/ceiling-fan-direction-summer-and-winter/">ceiling fan direction in summer and winter</a> suggests a quick screw check every time you flip the switch.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical symptom</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fix time</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Loose bracket or canopy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wobble at all speeds, canopy shifts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-15 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unrated or loose box</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Box moves when you push the fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2 hrs, or an electrician</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Loose blade screws</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gradual onset, sometimes clicking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bent blade iron</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One blade sits visibly lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-30 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Blade tracking off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wobble worst on high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weight imbalance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wobble on high only, mount solid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-40 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Downrod or hanger ball</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fan hangs slightly off vertical</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 min</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Everything below assumes you have switched the fan off at the wall and, for anything involving the canopy or the box, at the breaker as well. Use a proper stepladder rather than a chair, and do not run the fan while you are standing within reach of the blades.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bracket-canopy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Check the Mounting Bracket and Canopy First
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mounting bracket is the steel plate screwed to the electrical box that the whole fan hangs from, and the canopy is the decorative cover that hides it against the ceiling. On almost every modern fan the canopy is held by two or three small screws, and once those are out it slides down the downrod and exposes the bracket. This is the single most important place to look, and it takes about ten minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            With the canopy down, grip the bracket and try to move it. It should not shift at all. The bracket is normally fixed to the box with two machine screws, and those screws need to be tight with any lock washers in place. A bracket that rocks even slightly will translate into a wobble at the blades, because a few millimetres of movement at the ceiling becomes a much larger arc at the end of a downrod and a 52-inch blade sweep. Tighten the screws firmly but stop before you strip the threads in the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look at what the bracket is attached to, too. If the screws are going into a thin plastic box or into ceiling drywall rather than into the threaded ears of a metal or fan-rated box, stop there and read the next section before going further. A bracket screwed only to drywall is not a wobble problem; it is a fan that will eventually fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check the canopy itself. If it was cocked to one side when installed, it can touch the downrod or the hanger ball and rub as the fan oscillates, which both adds to the wobble and makes a scraping noise people mistake for the motor. Reseat it so it sits evenly against the ceiling with an even gap all the way around the downrod, and snug its screws by hand only. Overtightening a canopy can crack it or pull it against the downrod, recreating the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            A useful test while the canopy is down: with the fan off, put one hand on the motor housing and push it gently side to side. Watch the bracket and box. If the fan moves but the bracket stays dead still, your mount is sound and the wobble is coming from lower down. If the bracket, the box or the ceiling around it moves with the fan, you have found your problem and nothing else on this list will fix it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electrical-box" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is the Electrical Box Actually Rated for a Fan?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the cause that turns a nuisance into a hazard. A ceiling fan is not a light fixture. It weighs more, typically somewhere between 15 and 35 pounds for a residential fan with a light kit, and it loads its mount dynamically, with constant vibration and a twisting reaction every time the motor starts. A standard light-fixture box, particularly the shallow plastic kind nailed to the side of a joist, is not designed for that and can loosen or pull out over months of running.
          </p>

          <p>
            The US National Electrical Code is specific about this. Under section 314.27(C), a box used as the sole support of a ceiling-suspended paddle fan must be listed and marked by its manufacturer as suitable for fan support, and it cannot support a fan heavier than 70 pounds. Boxes designed for fans heavier than 35 pounds must also be marked with the maximum weight they can carry. That is where the 35 and 70 pound figures on fan boxes come from, and our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">ceiling fan buying guide</a> makes the same point: the box rating matters as much as the fan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Checking yours is straightforward once the canopy and bracket are down. A fan-rated box will normally be stamped or labelled inside with wording such as acceptable for fan support and a weight limit, and is usually metal, fixed directly to a joist with screws or hung from a metal fan brace. A box marked not for fan support, a plain plastic box with no rating, or a box held only by the drywall, all need to be replaced before the fan goes back up. If the house was built before ceiling fans were common in that room, assume the box is a light box until you prove otherwise.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is a fan-rated box, either screwed directly into a joist or hung from a retrofit fan brace, which is an adjustable steel bar that slides through the existing hole and expands until its ends bite into the joists on either side. A good <a href="/best-stud-finder/">stud finder</a> will tell you where the joists run and how far apart they are before you cut anything, which matters because brace bars are sized for standard joist spacing. The parts typically cost less than a takeaway meal; the labour is an hour or two if the wiring is straightforward.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the box moves, the fan comes down. Nothing else on this list matters until it does not.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Balancing blades on a fan hung from an unrated box makes it quieter, not safer. Swap the box first.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are not comfortable working with the wiring, this is the one cause on the list worth paying an electrician for. Replacing a box is routine work, the cost is driven mostly by ceiling access, whether there is an attic above, and whether the old box has to be cut out, and it removes the only failure mode here that can actually hurt someone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blade-screws-irons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Loose Blade Screws and Bent Blade Irons
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once the mount is proven solid, move to the blades. Each blade is held to a metal arm, usually called a blade iron or blade holder, by two or three screws, and each iron is in turn screwed to the motor's flywheel. Both sets of screws work loose over time, especially on blades made of MDF or plywood, which compress slightly with seasonal humidity changes. A single loose screw lets one blade shift its angle and its weight as the fan spins, and that alone can produce an obvious wobble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Go round every blade and check every screw. Tighten them firmly but carefully: the blade screws on most residential fans thread into wood composite, and overtightening strips the hole, which leaves you with a blade that can never be held tight again. If a screw spins without biting, a slightly longer screw of the same thread or a wooden toothpick and wood glue in the hole will usually rescue it. Check the screws holding the irons to the motor as well, since those are the ones people forget.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at the irons themselves. A blade iron that has been bent, even slightly, changes the pitch or height of its blade relative to the others. It happens during installation when blades are attached and then used as handles, during shipping, and very often during cleaning when someone pushes down on a blade tip with a duster. Hunter lists bent blade irons as one of the standard causes of wobble, and they are the most common reason a fan that was balanced at the factory wobbles in your house.
          </p>

          <p>
            The quickest way to spot a bent iron is visual. Stand directly under the fan, then off to one side at blade height, and compare the angle of each blade against its neighbours. A blade that droops lower at the tip, or that sits at a visibly steeper or flatter pitch, almost certainly has a bent iron. The next section gives you a precise way to measure it, but often your eye catches it first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some fans use one-piece blade arms that are cast rather than stamped, and those should not be bent back. If one of those is distorted, contact the manufacturer for a replacement arm, which most will supply. Never mix blades or irons from a different fan, even an identical model, without checking: blades are often supplied as weight-matched sets, and an odd one can introduce exactly the imbalance you are trying to remove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blade-tracking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Measure Blade Tracking With a Yardstick
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade tracking is the term for whether every blade tip passes the same point at the same height. If one blade rides a quarter of an inch lower than the rest, the fan wobbles regardless of how well matched the blades are by weight. Measuring it takes nothing more than a yardstick or a long tape measure and about fifteen minutes, and it is the step that separates a proper fix from an afternoon of guessing with balancing clips.
          </p>

          <p>
            With the fan off, hold the yardstick vertically against the ceiling so it lines up with the outer edge of one blade near the tip. Note the distance from the ceiling to the blade's edge, or simply mark the stick. Then turn the fan slowly by hand so the next blade comes round to the same spot, keeping the stick in exactly the same place, and measure again. Repeat for every blade. It is important to measure at the same point each time, so rotate the blades to the stick, not the stick to the blades.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tolerance fan makers work to is small. GE's manuals call for every blade to be within about 1/8 inch, or 3 mm, of the others. If all of your measurements fall inside that range, tracking is not your problem and you can move straight to balancing. If one blade is clearly out, that blade's iron is bent or its screws are loose, and fixing it will often eliminate the wobble entirely without any weights.
          </p>

          <p>
            Correcting a low or high blade on a stamped steel iron means bending the iron back very gently by hand. This Old House's guidance is honest about the limits: you cannot move it much, but there is usually enough play to bring a blade back into line. Remove the blade if that gives you better grip on the iron, make a small adjustment, refit, and measure again. Small movements and repeated measurement work far better than one big push, which tends to overshoot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once tracking is within 1/8 inch, run the fan on each speed and watch. A surprising number of wobbly fans are completely fixed at this stage, which is why it is worth doing before the balancing kit even comes out of the drawer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="balancing-kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Correct Weight Imbalance With a Balancing Kit
          </h2>

          <p>
            If the mount is solid, the screws are tight and the tracking is within tolerance, but the fan still wobbles on high, you have a weight imbalance. One blade is fractionally heavier than the others, or carries its weight further from the centre. The fix is the small balancing kit that comes with most fans, a plastic clip and a strip of adhesive weights. If yours has gone missing, kits cost a few dollars at any hardware store and are effectively universal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the blades first. Dust builds up unevenly, heavier on the leading edge and heavier on blades nearest a window or vent, and it adds real weight. Balancing a dirty fan means you will be chasing a moving target the next time it is wiped down. Then slide the clip onto the trailing edge of one blade, roughly halfway between the blade holder and the tip, and run the fan on high. Note whether the wobble gets better, worse or stays the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stop the fan, move the clip to the same position on the next blade, and repeat until you have tried every blade. The blade where the clip produces the biggest improvement is the light one that needs weight. On that blade, slide the clip in small steps toward the tip and back toward the motor, running the fan each time, until you find the position where the wobble is smallest. Hunter's own instructions describe exactly this process of small increments and repeated test runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you have found the spot, peel one of the adhesive weights and stick it on the top of the blade, on the blade's centre line, directly in line with where the clip was. Remove the clip and test again. If the wobble is reduced but not gone, the same blade may need a second weight beside the first, or a second blade may need a smaller correction; repeat the clip test on the remaining blades with the first weight in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            A coin taped to the top of a blade works in a pinch and follows the same logic, but it is heavier than a kit weight and easier to overcorrect with. Either way, if you have added two or three weights and the wobble is barely changed, stop. The imbalance is probably not a weight problem, and you should go back and recheck the mount and the tracking rather than covering the blade in weights.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="downrod-normal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Downrod, Ball Joint, and When Wobble Is a Safety Issue
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most downrod fans hang from a hanger ball at the top of the downrod that sits in a cup in the mounting bracket. The ball lets the fan find its own vertical, but it must be seated properly: most hanger balls have a slot or groove that has to engage a tab in the bracket, which stops the fan from rotating and keeps it centred. If the slot is not on the tab, the fan hangs slightly off axis and wobbles at every speed. Lift the fan slightly, rotate the ball until it drops into place, and you will often feel it settle.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the other end, check where the downrod meets the motor. It is normally held by a cross pin through the rod, a retaining clip on that pin, and one or two set screws in the motor collar. A missing clip or loose set screw lets the motor tilt on the rod. Tighten the set screws firmly, make sure the pin goes fully through and the clip is on, and check that the downrod is threaded fully into the hanger ball where it is threaded. Do not run any fan without its pin and clip, which are part of the safety system, not a convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Downrod length plays a part too. A longer rod amplifies any imbalance, so a fan that looks steady on a 6-inch rod can look restless on a 36-inch one. If your ceiling is sloped, check the bracket's slope rating in the manual and use the maker's angled adapter if the pitch exceeds it, because a hanger ball forced into a cup at too steep an angle can bind against the canopy. Sizing the fan and rod correctly in the first place avoids a lot of this, and our guide to <a href="/what-size-ceiling-fan-do-i-need/">what size ceiling fan you need</a> covers rod lengths for common ceiling heights.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When wobble means stop using the fan</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the fan off at the breaker and leave it off if any of these are true: the box or bracket moves when you push the motor, the wobble has been getting steadily worse over days or weeks, there is a new clicking, knocking or grinding noise, a blade or blade iron is cracked, the ceiling around the canopy is cracking or sagging, or the fan is hung from a box not rated for fan support. None of those are balancing problems, and every one of them can end with the fan on the floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have worked through every step and a fan still wobbles noticeably beyond 1/8 inch, the remaining suspects are a warped blade, which happens to MDF blades in humid rooms like bathrooms and covered porches, or a motor with a defect. Warped blades can usually be replaced as a matched set from the manufacturer. If the fan is old, cheap or out of warranty, replacement is often the more sensible spend, and a well-balanced DC motor fan from our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">tested ceiling fan picks</a> will run quieter and steadier than a tired budget fan you keep fighting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Balancing before checking the mount</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clipping weights onto a fan hung from a loose bracket or unrated box can calm the wobble enough that you stop noticing the real problem. Always prove the bracket and box are solid first, by pushing on the stationary motor and watching the mount, before touching a balancing kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overtightening blade screws into MDF</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most residential blades are wood composite, and a screw driven too hard strips its hole permanently. Tighten firmly, then stop. If a screw already spins freely, rescue the hole with a toothpick and wood glue or a slightly longer screw rather than cranking harder, which only makes the hole bigger.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring tracking by moving the yardstick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tracking only works if every blade is measured at exactly the same point under the ceiling. Keep the stick still and turn the blades to it by hand. Moving the stick between blades introduces errors bigger than the 1/8-inch tolerance you are checking against, and you end up bending an iron that was fine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Balancing dirty blades</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dust collects unevenly and adds real weight to some blades. Balance a dusty fan and the weights you add are correcting dirt, so the wobble returns the first time someone cleans it. Wipe every blade, top and leading edge, before you start the clip test.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving off the downrod pin or clip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cross pin and its retaining clip at the motor end are part of what holds the fan up, not trim. A fan running on set screws alone can tilt, wobble and eventually drop. If either is missing, get a replacement from the manufacturer or a hardware store before the fan runs again.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it normal for a ceiling fan to wobble a little?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, a small amount is normal. Manufacturers generally treat motor housing movement of around 1/8 inch on high speed as acceptable, and fans on long downrods sway more than flush-mount huggers. Wobble you can see as a clear circle, wobble at low speed, or wobble with clicking or a shifting canopy is beyond normal and worth diagnosing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a wobbling ceiling fan fall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wobble from blade imbalance alone rarely brings a fan down, but it does loosen screws over time. The real risk is wobble caused by a loose bracket or an electrical box that is not rated for fan support, because those can pull free. If the box or bracket moves when you push the stationary fan, stop using it until it is fixed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my electrical box is fan-rated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Take down the canopy and look inside the box. A fan-rated box is normally marked as acceptable for fan support with a maximum weight, and under the electrical code boxes for fans over 35 pounds must state their limit, up to 70 pounds. Plain plastic boxes, boxes marked not for fan support, or boxes held only by drywall need replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my fan only wobble on high speed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Imbalance forces rise sharply with speed, so a small weight or tracking difference that is invisible on low becomes obvious on high. If the mount is solid, wobble that only appears on high is almost always a blade tracking or weight problem. Measure tracking with a yardstick first, then use the balancing clip on each blade.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I balance a ceiling fan without a balancing kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A coin taped to the top of a blade does the same job as a kit weight, using the same trial process: tape it to each blade in turn, find the blade where it helps most, then slide it along to find the best spot. It is heavier than a kit weight, so it is easier to overcorrect. Kits are inexpensive and more precise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my fan start wobbling after cleaning it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cleaning is one of the most common triggers. Pressing down on a blade tip with a duster can bend the blade iron, and wiping can loosen blade screws or remove dust unevenly. Check every blade screw, then measure blade tracking with a yardstick. A bent iron usually shows up as one blade sitting lower than the others.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does reversing the fan direction cause wobble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Reversing direction does not create imbalance by itself, but the switch is often when people notice it, and the change in load can reveal screws that have worked loose over the season. Tightening blade and canopy screws at each seasonal reversal is good practice, and our guide to ceiling fan direction covers when to switch.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

