        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most ceiling fan wobble comes down to a loose screw, a bent blade iron or one blade a few grams light, and all three are fixed in under an hour with a screwdriver, a yardstick and a balancing kit. Work in order: bracket and box first, then blade screws, then tracking, then weight. The order is what keeps you from balancing a fan that is quietly working its way out of the ceiling.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If the box is not fan-rated, replace it before anything else. If the fan is old and still wobbles after every fix, it has probably earned its retirement, and the steadier DC models in our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">ceiling fan guide</a> are where we would start.
          </p>
